# ── Android App Links verification ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Serve the verification file at its required path (/.well-known/assetlinks.json)
# from a NON-hidden root file, so a drag-and-drop deploy — which silently omits
# hidden dot-folders — still publishes it.
# 200 = transparent rewrite (no HTTP redirect), which Android's verifier requires.
/.well-known/assetlinks.json   /assetlinks.json   200

# ── Tidy per-app URLs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Privacy, terms, deletion and support are ONE page each covering both apps, with
# a ?app= selector. These aliases mean the nested form works too, so a store
# listing, an email or an old link can use either shape:
#     /tutors/privacy  ==  /privacy?app=tutor
/tutors/privacy          /privacy?app=tutor          301
/tutors/terms            /terms?app=tutor            301
/tutors/delete-account   /delete-account?app=tutor   301
/tutors/support          /support?app=tutor          301
/tutors/contact          /support?app=tutor          301

# Singular and near-miss spellings of the tutor page — cheap to support, and these
# are the URLs people type after hearing the name said out loud.
/tutor        /tutors   301
/tutoring     /tutors   301
/for-tutors   /tutors   301

# Common aliases for the support page.
/contact   /support   301
/help      /support   301

# ── Tutor browser sign-in ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The tutor web app is served from this same site at /tutors/app/ (assembled by
# tools/build_site.sh). /tutors/login is kept as a stable alias because it is
# already linked from elsewhere and reads better when said out loud.
#
# No SPA rewrite is needed: the app uses hash routing (/tutors/app/#/practice/…),
# so deep links never reach the server.
/tutors/login    /tutors/app/    301
/tutor-login     /tutors/app/    301

# Student web app. Same courtesy as the tutor ones above: /app/ is the real
# path, but nobody types a trailing slash and "login" is what people guess.
/login           /app/           301
/log-in          /app/           301
/student-login   /app/           301

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# schools.theplanur.co.uk — the school presence on its own host
#
# It is a DOMAIN ALIAS of this same Netlify site, not a second deploy: one
# dist/, one drag, no duplicated CSS or fonts. Netlify matches absolute URLs in
# this file, so the host can be routed without a second project.
#
# 🔴 ORDER MATTERS. The root rule must come before the catch-all, because `/*`
# matches `/` as well.
#
# 🔴 WHY EVERY OTHER PATH IS SENT BACK TO THE APEX — this is the important one.
# An alias serves the WHOLE site on both hosts, so `schools.theplanur.co.uk`
# would happily serve /tutors/app/ too. That looks harmless and is not: a
# different host is a different ORIGIN, so the app would get its own
# localStorage and a tutor signed in on theplanur.co.uk would appear signed out
# on schools.theplanur.co.uk. Two sessions, silently — the same class of bug
# tutor_app's _webSessionKey exists to fix, arriving by a different route.
#
# Sending everything but the school page back to the apex means the app, the
# privacy notice and the terms exist in exactly ONE place. It also removes the
# duplicate-content problem an alias would otherwise create.
# ⏸️ PARKED 2026-09-05 — owner: build this WITH tutor.theplanur.co.uk, once a
# school is actually being onboarded. Uncomment all three lines together, and
# only AFTER the DNS record resolves and the certificate covers the host.
# Deploying these against a host that does not resolve gives a browser-level
# "server not found" — the same trap tutor.theplanur.co.uk is already stuck in.
# See README → "Subdomains, parked".
#
# https://schools.theplanur.co.uk/     /schools/index.html                200!
# https://schools.theplanur.co.uk/*    https://theplanur.co.uk/:splat     301!
# /schools                             https://schools.theplanur.co.uk/   301!
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Schools. Same app as tutors — a school is a large practice, not a second
# product — so every school entry point lands in the one tutor bundle.
#
# `?for=school` is read by the app to word its SIGN-IN SCREEN as "Planur for
# Schools". It is cosmetic and must stay that way: it is a query parameter, so
# anyone can set it, and nothing may ever be gated on it. See
# tutor_app/lib/core/config/entry_point.dart.
#
# ⚠️ A school-flavoured URL in the ADDRESS BAR is not achievable this way. The
# base href is compiled into the bundle, so keeping /schools/app/ visible needs
# a second Flutter build — and on one origin a second build shares the stored
# session unless it also carries its own persistSessionKey. Owner chose one
# door, 2026-09-03.
/school          /schools                           301
/schools/login   /tutors/app/?for=school        301
/school-login    /tutors/app/?for=school        301
/schools/app/*   /tutors/app/:splat?for=school  301
